{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"23529d09-2ef2-430b-ad19-12948d415725","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Consider a block on a frictionless incline at thirty degrees. Start by isolating the block and identifying the surfaces touching it.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Block on a frictionless incline"},{"op":"figure","id":"incline","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-3,3],"y":[-2.6,2.2],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"ramp","from":[-2.8,-2.078],"to":[2.8,1.155]},{"kind":"polyline","id":"block","points":[[-0.406,-0.696],[0.806,0.004],[0.406,0.696],[-0.806,-0.004]],"closed":true},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0]},{"kind":"segment","from":[-2.8,-2.078],"to":[-1.3,-2.078],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"angle","at":[-2.8,-2.078],"from":[-1.3,-2.078],"to":[2.8,1.155],"r":0.25,"label":"30°"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"23529d09-2ef2-430b-ad19-12948d415725","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Only two forces act: weight vertically downward and the normal force perpendicular to the incline. Frictionless means there is no friction force.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"forces","on":"incline","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"weight","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[0,-2],"arrow":true,"label":"mg"},{"kind":"segment","id":"normal","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[-0.866,1.5],"arrow":true,"label":"N"}]},{"op":"text","id":"force_note","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"content":"Weight: down. Normal: perpendicular."}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"23529d09-2ef2-430b-ad19-12948d415725","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Resolve weight along and into the slope. These dashed components replace weight in calculations; they are not additional forces.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"components","on":"incline","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"parallel","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[-0.866,-0.5],"arrow":true,"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","id":"inward","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[0.866,-1.5],"arrow":true,"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","from":[-0.866,-0.5],"to":{"on":"weight","t":1},"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","from":[0.866,-1.5],"to":{"on":"weight","t":1},"stroke":"dashed"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"resolve","anchor":{"el":"force_note","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"W_{\\parallel}=mg\\sin30^\\circ=\\frac{mg}{2}"},{"op":"equation","id":"perp","anchor":{"el":"resolve","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"W_{\\perp}=mg\\cos30^\\circ"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Which component of weight is balanced by the normal force?","expected_gist":"The component perpendicular to the incline."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"23529d09-2ef2-430b-ad19-12948d415725","step":{"id":"s4","script":"There is no acceleration through the surface, so normal force balances perpendicular weight. The unbalanced downhill component produces acceleration equal to half of g.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"balance","anchor":{"el":"perp","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"N=mg\\cos30^\\circ"},{"op":"equation","id":"accel","anchor":{"el":"balance","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"ma=mg\\sin30^\\circ\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a=\\frac{g}{2}"},{"op":"figure","id":"motion","on":"incline","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":[-0.8,0.8],"to":[-2.099,0.05],"arrow":true,"label":"a downhill"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
